// Inherent methods on the built-in sequences.
//
// `.len()` is **not** compiler syntax. It is an ordinary inherent method on the
// primitive types, declared here — an `impl` with no trait, on a type no other
// package is allowed to write one for — and marked `#intrinsic` (§6.4), so the
// compiler supplies what it does. That is what makes `a.len()` on a `[3]i32`,
// `s.len()` on a `[]i32`, and a future `Vector.len()` one spelling with one
// meaning, instead of a special case the compiler carries.
//
// The coherence rule is why these can only live in `core`: an inherent impl may
// only be written in the package that defines the type, and the structural types
// belong to `core` (§4.9).
//
// `Self` inside these impls is the structural target: `[]T` in the first, `[N]T`
// in the second. On a `[N]T` whose `N` is known, `len` folds to the literal
// count at compile time, so the inlined call costs nothing; on a `[]T` it reads
// the slice header's length.

{ Index, IndexMut } :: import "ops.nest"
{ transmute } :: import "mem.nest"

// The element count of an array or slice. This is the second intrinsic whose
// signature cannot state its own rule: `T` is unbounded here and the compiler
// rejects an argument that is neither an array nor a slice, because no bound in
// the language says "one of the two built-in sequences".
@public len :: #intrinsic("len") func <T> (x: T) -> usize

// The method **is** the intrinsic, rather than a body that calls one. Writing it
// the other way — `len :: func (self: *Self) -> usize { return len(self) }` —
// looks like it forwards to the namespace's `len` above and does not: inside an
// impl, `len` resolves to the impl's own member, so the body called itself.
//
// Marking the member `#intrinsic` is also the more honest spelling of what these
// are. There was never a body to write: `#intrinsic` means the compiler supplies
// one (§6.4), and a forwarding call was only ever a way of naming which one.
impl <T> []T {
  len :: #intrinsic("len") func (self: *Self) -> usize

  // The address the elements start at.
  //
  // A slice **is** a pointer and a length (`design/lir.md` §7b), so this is the
  // first of those two words rather than anything computed. It exists because
  // the alternative was every caller declaring its own two-word struct and
  // `transmute`-ing to it — which is the same reinterpretation, written out
  // once per caller and wrong in a way nothing would catch if the two words
  // ever swapped places.
  //
  // What comes back is a `*T`, so it is traced and non-null like any other, and
  // it is only as valid as the slice it came from. A slice of length zero still
  // has an address; there is simply nothing at it to read.
  @public ptr :: #inline func (self: Self) -> *T {
    return transmute.<Raw.<T>>(self).ptr
  }
}

// The two words a slice is, so that `ptr` above can name the first of them.
// Private: a program that wants the pointer calls `ptr`, and a program that
// wants to take a slice apart some other way is doing something `core` should
// not be handing out.
Raw :: struct <T> { ptr: *T, len: usize }

impl <T, const N: usize> [N]T {
  len :: #intrinsic("len") func (self: *Self) -> usize
}

// ===< Indexing >===
//
// `a[i]` is **not** compiler syntax either (§6.13): it is `Index.index(&a, i).*`
// for every type, the built-in sequences included. They are here rather than in
// the compiler for the same reason `.len()` is — one spelling with one meaning —
// and the members are `#intrinsic` for the same reason too: there is no body to
// write, only a compiler operation to name.
//
// The trait hands back a **pointer** to the element. That is what makes `a[i]` a
// *place*: `a[i] = v` writes through it, and a method returning the element by
// value could not be assigned to.
//
// There is deliberately **no `IndexMut`** here, and it is not an omission. A
// sequence's write permission is in its *type*, not in its receiver: a `[]mut T`
// is writable through however immutably the binding holding it was declared
// (§2.3, §3.2), and an array's elements belong to whatever holds the array.
// `IndexMut`'s `self: *mut Self` asks for permission over the *container*, which
// is the right question for a user container and the wrong one for these two —
// it would refuse `s[0] = 1` for every `s: []mut T` the program did not also
// declare `mut`. So a write goes through this impl too, and what the element
// pointer permits is the compiler's answer for the receiver it was given.
//
// One impl covers `[]T` and `[]mut T`: the permission is on the slice, not on
// which impl applies.

impl <T> Index.<usize> for []T {
  Output :: T
  index :: #intrinsic("index") func (self: *Self, i: usize) -> *T
}

impl <T, const N: usize> Index.<usize> for [N]T {
  Output :: T
  index :: #intrinsic("index") func (self: *Self, i: usize) -> *T
}

// ===< Byte equality >===

// Whether two byte slices hold the same bytes.
//
// It carries a `#lang` tag because the **compiler** needs it: a `match` on a
// `str` with a literal arm (`s.match { "hi" => ... }`) is a question about the
// bytes, and by the time the lowering sees it a `str` is a pointer and a length
// (`design/lir.md` §7b) — comparing those two words would compare *addresses*,
// which is not what the pattern asked. So the lowering emits a call to whatever
// carries this tag, exactly the way it emits a call to `#lang("panic")`.
//
// Writing it here rather than as an unrolled comparison in the compiler is what
// keeps one answer to "are these bytes equal": `impl Eq for str` calls this, the
// pattern calls this, and a program that replaces `core` replaces both together.
@public bytes_eq :: #lang("bytes_eq") func (a: []u8, b: []u8) -> bool {
  if a.len() != b.len() { return false }
  let mut i: usize := 0
  while i < a.len() {
    if a[i] != b[i] { return false }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return true
}
